rates: `DUP(h)` → h_b with `pos(it,h_b) := 1`, write 5; `$a = $b` through the loop reference: it now holds h_b (rc 2: `$b` and the reference); next fetch: copy hit on h_b → **no separation**; fetch h_b[1]: `MAKE_REF` on the shared slot, `$v` bound; `$v = 9` writes through; `break`; `$b[1]` reads h_b → **9** ✓.\n    25\t- Round 6's unrelated-table program is unaffected (no record on `$b`'s table): still **2**. Round 7's \"copy before the loop, assign back\" program: `FE_RESET_RW` registers the iterator after separating, so the pre-loop copy's table has no record → unrelated → separate → still restarts at `ptr`.\n    26\t\n    27\tFixtures (P3, `iteration-v1`): F1 and the reviewer's program verbatim; a chain copy adopted two iterations later (records survive same-table fetches: position carried, no separation); a copy whose table was mutated by `unset` before adoption (its record adjusted on that table); a copy made, then the source mutated, then the copy adopted (the record on the copy is the position at duplication, adjusted only by the copy's mutations); adoption of a shared table followed by a write through `$v` visible to the other holder.\n    28\t\n    29\t## 2. Named-argument validation at the send, after the argument's own evaluation (P1-2): `CHECK_FUNC_ARG`, `SEND_VAL_EX`/`SEND_VAR_EX`, `zend_handle_named_arg`\n    30\t\n    31\tZend compiles each argument as `CHECK_FUNC_ARG` (which looks the name up only to decide by-reference sending and **defers** an unknown name), then the argument expression's own opcodes, then the `SEND_*_EX` for it, where `zend_handle_named_arg` validates the name and raises `Unknown named parameter` or `Named parameter … overwrites previous argument`. So the offending argument's expression **runs**; later arguments do not. F2's first argument was a constant, so it could not show this; round 8's row 57 (\"before evaluating `expr`\") is corrected.\n    32\t\n    33\t| # | Construct (handler) | Rule |\n    34\t|---|---|---|\n    35\t| 57 (revised) | named argument `name: expr` | (1) `CHECK_FUNC_ARG`: look `name` up on the callee only to choose read versus write-fetch for `expr`; an unknown name is **not** an error here and `expr` is fetched for read. (2) Evaluate `expr` (side effects happen; the value is an owned or borrowed operand as rows 32 to 35). (3) At the send, validate: **unknown** and not variadic → `Error: Unknown named parameter $name`; **already supplied** → `Error: Named parameter $name overwrites previous argument`; on either, the just-evaluated value is released (borrowed: nothing; owned: `DELREF`) and pending-call cleanup (row 39) releases the arguments sent so far; later arguments never run. (4) Otherwise acquire and place by name as before (unknown on a variadic callee: collected under the string key) |\n    36\t| 63 to 65 | unchanged | entry-time defaults and `ArgumentCountError`; positional-after-named compile error; string-key spreads follow row 57's step 3 at the spread's send |\n    37\t\n    38\tTraces: **F2** `f(bad: 1, x: side())`: `1` evaluated, send `bad` → `Error`; `side()` never runs ✓. **Reviewer's program** `f(bad: side())`: `side()` runs (`seen`), send `bad` → `Error` ✓. `f(1, x: side())` with one parameter: `side()` runs, send `x` → overwrite `Error`.\n    39\t\n    40\tFixtures (`oracle-2026-10-17/F2` plus `calls-v1`): the three programs above; `f(bad: new D)` where `D` has a destructor echoing (constructed, then destroyed during cleanup before the `Error` surfaces, order pinned); `g(...)` variadic with `g(bad: side())` (runs, collected); a by-reference named parameter with an unknown name (`CHECK_FUNC_ARG` chooses read-fetch: the operand's missing key is not vivified; pinned by oracle); php-src `Zend/tests/named_params/unknown_named_param.phpt`, `overwrite_*`, `named_params_variadic*` as direct oracles.\n    41\t\n    42\t## 3. Readonly initialization clears the clone flag (P1-3): `zend_std_write_property` readonly branch\n    43\t\n    44\tZend's successful write to an uninitialized readonly property clears both the uninitialized state and the reinitializable flag (`Z_PROP_FLAG_P(slot) &= ~(IS_PROP_UNINIT | IS_PROP_REINITABLE)`). Round 8's row 66 left the flag set on initialization; corrected. F3 is unaffected: its `unset` already cleared the flag and the following write was an initialization.\n    45\t\n    46\t| # | Construct (handler) | Rule |\n    47\t|---|---|---|\n    48\t| 66 (revised) | write to a readonly property | **uninitialized**: initialization from the declaring scope, allowed; **the flag is cleared** (whether or not it was set). From another scope: `Error: Cannot initialize readonly property C::$x from scope …`. **initialized, `reinitable`**: allowed, flag cleared. **initialized, not `reinitable`**: `Error: Cannot modify readonly property C::$x` |\n    49\t| 67 | unchanged | `unset`: allowed when `reinitable` (clears it, property becomes uninitialized) or when uninitialized from the declaring scope; otherwise `Error: Cannot unset readonly property C::$x` |\n    50\t| 52, 68 | unchanged | flags set at `__clone` entry, cleared at exit; references to readonly always refused |\n    51\t\n    52\tTraces: **reviewer's program** (`x` never initialized, `__clone` writes twice): entry sets the flag; first write is an initialization → allowed, flag cleared; second write: initialized, no flag → `Error` ✓. **F3**: `unset` clears the flag and uninitializes; write initializes → `2` ✓. Two writes on an initialized property in `__clone`: first reinitializes (flag cleared), second `Error` ✓ (round 7).\n    53\t\n    54\tFixtures (`oracle-2026-10-17/F3` plus `objects-v1`): the reviewer's program; F3; `unset` then two writes in `__clone` (second `Error`); initialization outside `__clone` from the declaring scope on a never-initialized property followed by a second write (`Error`); php-src `Zend/tests/readonly_props/readonly_clone_*.phpt`, `initialization_scope_*.phpt`, `unset_*.phpt`.\n    55\t\n    56\t## 4. Bookkeeping\n    57\t\n    58\tRows changed this round: 25, 57, 60, 61 (replaced), 66. Fixture groups touched: `iteration-v1`, `calls-v1`, `objects-v1`. Corner table, slices, owner decisions: unchanged. Under the round-7 blocking rule these are claimed-row corrections, each with an oracle fixture to run before P3/P4 land.\n    59\t\n    60\t## What round 9's reviewer should probe\n    61\t\n    62\t1. **Copy-hit adoption of a shared table** (row 25): the slot `MAKE_REF` on adoption is visible to every holder of that table; a fixture where the other holder is then copied again (`$c = $b` after adoption) should show the reference preserved in `$c` by `DUP`'s rc>1 rule.\n    63\t2. **Record deletion on migration**: whether Zend deletes the non-adopted copies at migration or leaves them until `FE_FREE`; observable only if the loop later migrates *back* to a previously copied table (adopt again versus unrelated). One oracle program decides; row 25 says deleted (so a return is unrelated).\n    64\t3. **`CHECK_FUNC_ARG` with an unknown name on a by-reference-only callee** (`function f(&$x)`, `f(bad: $u['k'])`): read-fetch, no vivification, then the `Error`; pinned by oracle before landing.\n    65\t4. **Row 67's uninitialized-`unset` branch**, still oracle-pinned as in round 8.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
